  "documentation": {
    "range": "This species&#160;is distributed in the Indo-Pacific from the Red Sea and East Africa, eastward to the Line and Tuamotu Islands, northward to southern Japan, and southward to eastern and western Australia (Allen <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al.</span> 2003). It is not known to occur in the Persian Gulf, Oman or the Hawaiian Islands (Randall 2005). The depth range is 1-50 metres (Matsuura 2001).",
    "population": "This species is moderately common in the northern Bismarck Sea of Papua New Guinea (Hamilton <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al. </span>2009), Raja Ampat, Indonesia (Allen 2003), Kariyachalli Island in the Gulf of Mannar (Matthews <em>et al. </em>2015) and in Taiwan (K-T. Shao pers. comm. 2011). It was occasionally recorded in Milne Bay, Papua New Guinea (Allen 2003). In the central Philippines, there have been reports of decreasing catches from fishermen from a catch rate of 12 kg/day in the 1970s and 10 kg/day in the 1990s to zero catches in the early 2000s (Lavides <span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al.</span> 2010).",
    "habitats": "This species inhabits coral reefs from the lagoon to the lower slope. It is more common along the reef edge and upper slope (Anderson and Hafiz 1987). It is the largest of the triggerfishes reaching a total length of 75 cm. Prey items include sea urchins, coral, crabs, molluscs, and tube worms (Randall 2005). Nests have been observed in coral reef spurs between 3.7-15.2 meters (Clark <em>et al.</em> 2015).",
    "threats": "Exploitation impacts this species in parts of its range (e.g., Philippines; Lavides&#160;<span style=\"font-style: italic;\">et al.</span>&#160;2010). It may also be impacted by ongoing coral reef degradation.",
    "measures": "There are no species-specific conservation measures.",
    "use_trade": "This species is a targeted food fish in the Philippines and Indonesia where it is often found in fish markets (B. Stockwell pers. comm. 2011), as well as in the Solomon Islands (Molea and Vuki 2008). It is incidentally caught in the Ryukyu Islands and is a minor component of the aquarium trade.",
    "rationale": "This widely distributed reef species is common in parts of its range. It is exploited by fisheries in parts of its range and may be locally impacted by ongoing coral reef degradation. At this time, these threats are not suspected to cause a global level population decline nearing 30%; therefore, it is listed as Least Concern.",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
